2. As blood viscosity increases, the blood flow velocity decreases, as the blood is more resistant to moving through the blood vessels. This is because the blood flow velocity is inversely proportional to the viscosity of the blood.

3. As blood viscosity increases, the pressure required to maintain blood flow also increases. This is because the blood flow pressure is directly proportional to the viscosity of the blood. This increased pressure is necessary to overcome the increased resistance caused by the increased viscosity.

