   \]
   The limit of \( e^{-t/2} \) as \( t \to \infty \) is \( 0 \), since \( -t/2 \to -\infty \), and \( e^{-t/2} \to 0 \).

2. **Evaluate the limit of the second term:**
   \[
   \lim_{t \to \infty} \left[(1/3)\sin t + \cos t\right] = 0.
   \]
   This is because \( \sin t \) and \( \cos t \) are periodic functions with values between \(-1\) and \( 1 \), and \( e^{-t/2} \) approaches \( 0 \) as \( t \to \infty \).

