   The variance of \( X \) is zero. For a discrete random variable \( X \), the variance is zero if and only if \( X \) takes only one value, and this value must be the mean of \( X \). Thus, \( \text{Var}(X) = 0 \) implies that \( X \) takes only one value, and this value is \( \text{E}(X) \).

