","The entropy production rate \( \Pi_S \) is given by:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr}} v}{T}
\]
where:
- \( F_{\text{fr}} \) is the friction force,
- \( v \) is the mean relative velocity of one hand with respect to the other,
- \( T \) is the room temperature.

This result is derived from the Clausius relation for entropy production:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{1}{T} \dot{W}_{\text{diss}}
\]
where \( \dot{W}_{\text{diss}} \) is the dissipated power due to friction. The dissipated power is the product of the friction force and the relative velocity:
\[
\dot{W}_{\text{diss}} = F_{\text{fr}} v
\]
Thus, the entropy production rate is:
\[
\Pi_S = \frac{F_{\text{fr} v}}{T}
\]

